library(readr)
library(fpp2)
library(TTR)
Total_SA <- read_csv("TOTALSA.csv")
Total_Raw <- Total_SA$`Sales Units(in Millions)`
Total_Sales <- ts(Total_Raw, frequency = 12, start = c(2019,1))
plot(Total_Sales)
# Plot and Inference
# Time Series Plot
plot(Total_Sales,
main = paste("Time Series Plot for Total Sales"),
xlab = "Year",
ylab = "Sales Unites (in Millions)",
col = "black",
lwd = 2)
# Observation: The total vehicle sales prior to 2020 was steady rising and falling between 16 and 18 million units which are nor,al pre-pandemic sales levels. However, the onset of COVID-19 pandemic lead to factory shutdowns, supply chain disruptions, dealership closures, reduced demand due to people either losing their jobs or working from home caused a sharp drop in number of units sold. Towards the end of 2020 into 2021 was the recovery period where the sales once again peaked near 18-19 million unites due to the economy stabilizing, stimulus checks, and there was a temporary surge in demand for cars. In 2022, there is once again a sharp drop due to the supply chain issues, inflation, and rising interest rates causing the sales to drop around 13-14 million units. Post the 2022 drop, there has been a consistent recovery in the sales of vehicles due to production stabilization, recovery of the consumer demand and back to work arrangements, however this recovery period displays an entirely different trend which is not captured by the past data. Therefore, in this case, the data from mid-2021 and onwards will be used for the upcoming forecasting.
time_series <- window(Total_Sales, start = c(2022,2))
plot(time_series)
# Central Tendency
summary(time_series)
boxplot(time_series,
main = "Boxplot for the Total Sales of Vehicles",
ylab = "Value")
# Observation: By removing the older data, the recent data that would have become the lower level outliers are now being displayed as the data to be forecasted. This boxplot will roughly create a normal distribution, however since the median is closer to the top of the boxplot, the distribution is slightly skewed. In this case, the distribution will be negatively-skewed AKA left-skewed. IQR = 1.78 millions, showing the data is more concentrated (tightly clustered) and there is moderate variability around the median level. This variation suggests "typical" car sales variation.
# Decomposition
stl_decomp <- stl(time_series,s.window ="periodic")
plot(stl_decomp)
# The time series is seasonal because it does have seasonal fluctuations displayed by the spikes at certain time intervals followed by a pattern of decline, incline, and plateau and then repeating the pattern again.
decomp_total_sales <- decompose(time_series)
decomp_total_sales
# The decomposition is additive.
monthly_indices <- decomp_total_sales$seasonal[1:12]
monthly_indices
# High: March (0.667956597 million units)
# Low: November (-1.170501736 million units)
# November has the lowest monthly index (-1.170501736 million units) indicating a seasonal dip because it is the month that starts the winter, which leads to less people going into dealerships and test driving cars which reduces purchase, especially with the onset of holiday season more people are less inclined to spend their month buying automobiles due to restrictive financial freedom. There are also less promotions in this month.
# March has the highest monthly index (0.667956597 million units) because it is the start of spring indicating the start of buying season which indicates the onset of promotions leading up to the summer months. March also marks the month when some people start receiving their tax-refunds, which indicates little bit of financial freedom to purchase automobiles.
seasadj <- seasadj(stl_decomp)
seasadj
plot(seasadj)
lines(time_series, col="Red")
# Seasonality does have big fluctuations in the values of the time series, it amplifies the spikes and the declines in certain months each year.
# Naive Method
naive_model <- naive(time_series)
plot(naive_model)
checkresiduals(naive_model)
naive_residuals <- residuals(naive_model)
naive_residuals
plot(naive_residuals,
main = "Residuals from Naive Forecast for Car Sales",
ylab = "Residuals", xlab = "Time",
col = "blue", type = "l")
abline(h = 0, col = "red")
# Since the plot fluctuates around 0 the naive model seems to have no strong bias, it does not consistently overpredicts or underpredicts the data. However, there are certain months where the model's predictions were off potentially due to unexpected events, since the model takes the last value to forecast it can be said that it does not capture the short-term volatility in car sales.
hist(naive_residuals,
main = "Histogram of Residuals",
xlab = "Residuals",
col = "lightblue")
# The residuals are clustered around 0 so the model's errors balance out which supports the earlier observation that the model does not have a strong bias. However, as observed from the boxplot there is a slight skewness to the left, meaning that the model occasionally overpredicts more strongly than it underpredicts. Since the histogram is not perfect bell-shaped, it means that the model probably fails to capture more seasonal/sudden variations.
plot(as.numeric(fitted(naive_model)), residuals(naive_model),
main = "Residuals vs Fitted Values",
xlab = "Fitted Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "steelblue", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# Residuals vs. Fitted plot shows mostly randomly scattered points indicating that the model fits the systematic trends, and the variance of errors is mainly constant.
plot(as.numeric(time_series), residuals(naive_model),
main = "Residuals vs Actual Values",
xlab = "Actual Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "navy", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# The randomly scattered points indicate that the model fits the data well, the variance of errors is roughly constant, and there is no systematic overprediction or underprediction pattern exists in the model.
Acf(naive_residuals)
# The ACF plot after the 1st lag remains between the blue dashed line or the 95% confidence interval indicating there is no significant autocorrelation and the model has captured all systematic patterns and the variation we see is random white noise. The ACF plot exceeding the blue lines in the initial lag could be the recovery period after the second dip in the time series indicating some external shocks left uncaptured by the model.
accuracy(naive_model)
naive_forecast <- naive(time_series,12)
naive_forecast
plot(naive_forecast)
# The MAPE for this type of forecast is 3.348253 which is less than 10%, so this is a good indicator that the naive forecast is overall performing well.
# In 1 year, the time series value for the total car sales will be 16.191 million units.
# The forecast is taking the last value and predicting the values for the next whole year for the point level forecast. However, in order to understand inventory, staffing and resource allocation to manage the rise in total car sales, high 80-95% range of 19.06691 - 20.58933 million units should be considered.
# Simple Moving Averages
plot(time_series,
main = paste("Time Series Plot for Total Car Sales"),
xlab = "Year",
ylab = "Total Car Sales",
col = "black")
MA3_forecast <- ma(time_series,order=3)
lines(MA3_forecast, col = "red")
MA6_forecast <- ma(time_series,order=6)
lines(MA6_forecast, col = "blue")
MA9_forecast <- ma(time_series,order=9)
lines(MA9_forecast, col = "green")
# It is observed that the moving average of the order 9 is the best fit that has the strongest smoothing out of the 3. So in forecasting the next 12 months, it will be the best order fit to be used.
# Moving Average with the Order = 9
MA9_model <- ma(time_series, order = 9, centre = FALSE)
MA9_f <- forecast(MA9_model, h = 12)
MA9_f
plot(MA9_f)
accuracy(MA9_f)
# Based on the moving average of order 9 forecast, in 1 year the total car sales will be 15.75691 million units.
MA3_model <- ma(time_series, order = 3, centre = FALSE)
MA3_f <- forecast(MA3_model, h = 12)
MA3_f
plot(MA3_f)
accuracy(MA3_f)
MA6_model <- ma(time_series, order = 6, centre = FALSE)
MA6_f <- forecast(MA6_model, h = 12)
MA6_f
plot(MA6_f)
accuracy(MA6_f)
# As the moving average order goes up, the smoothing is stronger. At the 9th order, it produces a smoother trend without seasonality and noise. It has the best fit and lowest errors. This is also proved by the MAPE as it moves from 1.012378 -> 0.572978 -> 0.5147158 for order 3 to order 9, respectively. A MAPE below 0 means that it is statistically an extremely close prediction with extremely accurate relative error. Any of the moving average orders past 3rd order will be great for long-term forecasting considering their MAPE values.
# Simple Smoothing
ses_model <- ses(time_series,12)
plot(ses_model)
summary(ses_model)
# The value of alpha is 0.5494. It gives 55% weight to the latest observations indicating moderate level of smoothing, the model responds fairly quickly to recent changes in data. The model in this cases balances between stability and responsiveness. 55% weight to the recent data indicates some variability but not too much noise.
# The initial state value is 14.1881 million units.
# The value of sigma is 0.5852. Sigma gives the typical deviation of actual values from fitted values. Based on the initial state, the forecast deviates from the actual values by 4.12% which is considered to be a good forecast. The deviation in this case is considered low meaning the model fits the data very closely and the predictions are quite stable. Therefore it can be used for practical predictions centered around the total car units sold.
checkresiduals(ses_model)
ses_residuals <- residuals(ses_model)
ses_residuals
plot(ses_residuals,
main = "Residuals from Simple Exponential Smoothing Forecast for Total Car Sales",
ylab = "Residuals", xlab = "Time",
col = "blue", type = "l")
abline(h = 0, col = "red")
# The plot is centered around 0, however the sharp spikes on both sides in the residuals plot indicate that the trend and seasonality still exist in this forecast model.
hist(ses_residuals,
main = "Histogram of Residuals",
xlab = "Residuals",
col = "seagreen")
# The residuals are clustered around 0 so the model's errors balance out, however, as observed from the boxplot there is a slight skewness to the left, meaning that the model occasionally overpredicts more strongly than it underpredicts. Since the histogram is not a perfect bell-shaped, it means that the model probably fails to capture some of the seasonal/sudden variations.
plot(as.numeric(fitted(ses_model)), residuals(ses_model),
main = "Residuals vs Fitted Values",
xlab = "Fitted Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "steelblue", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# Residuals vs. Fitted plot shows mostly randomly scattered points indicating that the model fits the systematic trends, and the variance of errors is mainly constant. However, some clustering is observed around 13.5 and 16 million units and this can indicate model misfit due to heteroscedasticity AKA variance of residuals is not constant across these range of fitted values.
plot(as.numeric(time_series), residuals(ses_model),
main = "Residuals vs Actual Values",
xlab = "Actual Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "navy", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# The randomly scattered points indicate that the model fits the data fairly well, however the variance of residuals is roughly constant except for around 14.25 and 16 million units and values above that.
Acf(ses_residuals)
# The ACF plot remains between the blue dashed line or the 95% confidence interval indicating there is no significant autocorrelation and the model has captured all systematic patterns and the variation we see is random white noise.
accuracy(ses_model)
ses_forecast <- ses(time_series,12)
ses_forecast
plot(ses_forecast)
# The MAPE for this type of forecast is 2.762092 which is less than 5%. The model predicts total car sales within ±2.8% of actual values on average which is an excellent level of accuracy. The mean and variance in this case have no strong trend or seasonality and the alpha is well-tuned to balance stability and responsiveness like discussed earlier.
# In 1 year, the time series value for the total car sales will be 16.02267 million units.
# Based on the MAPE, we can use the forecasted value of 16.02267 million units of sales to understand funding and resource allocation for the car manufacturing. There will be a large amount of people allocating their income to buying a car in the upcoming year, therefore the car manufacturing companies and dealerships should accordingly staff and allocate finances towards car manufacturing and sales departments.
# Holt-Winters
HW_model <- HoltWinters(time_series)
HW_model
plot(HW_model)
# Alpha = 0.3621374, indicates moderate smoothing so the model in this case gives 36% weight to the latest observations and the rest comes from past smoothed values. The model in this case is semi-reactive to new changes.
# Beta = 0; The beta component indicates a fixed slope (linear trend from initial estimate). It behaves like simple exponential smoothing with an added seasonal term.
# Gamma = 0; indicates seasonal smoothing which in this case is also constant and does not update over time. The seasonal pattern is stable.
# a = 16.40149593 million units is the initial level of the series; it is an average non-seasonalized value at start.
# b = 0.15987777 is the initial trend aka slope. This is a positive trend which states that per period there is an increase of 0.15987777 units on the top of the 'a' value above. However, since the beta is 0, the trend does not evolve and it stays constant.
# s1 - s12 values are the seasonal indices for months 1 to 12. These represent the additive seasonal adjustments relative to the baseline. For certain months, the value is positive indicating an above average value and for others, the value is negative indicating a below average value.
checkresiduals(HW_model)
HW_model$sigma # Value outputted is NULL
HW_residuals <- residuals(HW_model)
HW_residuals
sigma <- sd(HW_residuals, na.rm = TRUE) # Helps manually extract the sigma
sigma
# Sigma = 0.8383126; Based on the alpha value, the model deviates by about 0.8383126 units, which means that the model relative error is 5.11%. Since the forecasting model relative error is under 10%, the model is reasonably accurate and most forecasts are within +/- 5.11% of the actual values. When it comes down to inventory, budget, and resource planning to accommodate the total car sales, this level of standard deviation is acceptable and the car manufacturers does not be concerned based on this sigma.
plot(HW_residuals,
main = "Residuals from Holt-Winters Forecast for the Total Car Sales",
ylab = "Residuals", xlab = "Time",
col = "blue", type = "l")
abline(h = 0, col = "red")
# Early part of 2023, there is slight downward drift where the model overpredicts those values and it thinks that the sales were higher than they were. Then it shifts to fluctuate around 0 which is relatively stable. There are no consistent seasonal or cyclical patterns repeating in residuals — meaning the seasonality was captured reasonably well. There is moderate deviation overall, however large residual spikes near the end could be possibly because of structural shift in the data due to supply, policy, or demand change.
hist(HW_residuals,
main = "Histogram of Residuals",
xlab = "Residuals",
col = "lightblue")
# The residuals are mostly random and centered around 0 indicating no systematic bias. The HW model fits well overall. In few cases the model overpredicted (actual < forecast) indicating slight left-skewness to the data.
plot(fitted(HW_model), residuals(HW_model),
main = "Residuals vs Fitted Values",
xlab = "Fitted Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "steelblue", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# The xhat plot shows the predicted values but it closely follows the shape of the actual data, therefore it can be interpreted that the model captures the overall pattern well. The level plot red line shows the baseline level component, the blue line represents the increasing level component. The slightly rising line means that the total car sales is gradually increasing which is consistent with the alpha of 0.36. The trend is constant and the seasonality is also constant based on the observed beta and gamma values earlier.
plot(as.numeric(tail(time_series, length(residuals(HW_model)))), residuals(HW_model),
main = "Residuals vs Actual Values",
xlab = "Actual Values",
ylab = "Residuals",
col = "navy", pch = 19)
abline(h = 0, col = "red", lwd = 2)
# This plot is randomly scattered so the model captures the main trend of the data. The errors are independent so there are no systematic bias and the model's variance is stable. The residuals in this case look like white noise.
Acf(HW_residuals)
# The ACF plot remains between the blue dashed line or the 95% confidence interval indicating there is no significant autocorrelation and the model has captured all systematic patterns and the variation we see is random white noise.
HW_forecast <- forecast(HW_model,12)
HW_forecast
accuracy(HW_forecast)
plot(HW_forecast)
# The MAPE for this type of forecast is 3.678747 which is less than 5% so this is a good indicator that the Holt-Winters forecast method is performing extremelt well. Considering the trend and seasonal component, this model can be used to model our dataset.
# In 1 year, the time series value for the total car sales will be 18.26062 million units.
# The forecast is mimicking the original plot, so it is adding some noise back into the forecast, therefore the forecast takes the trend of the overall plot of the actual dataset as it looks to forecast the upcoming year.
# Accuracy Summary
Naive <- accuracy(naive_model)
MA3 <- accuracy(MA3_f)
MA6 <- accuracy(MA6_f)
MA9 <- accuracy(MA9_f)
SES <- accuracy(ses_model)
HoltWinters <- accuracy(HW_forecast)
models <- list(
Naive = Naive,
MA3 = MA3,
MA6 = MA6,
MA9 = MA9,
SES = SES,
HoltWinters = HoltWinters
)
accuracy_table <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(models), function(name) {
acc <- models[[name]][1, c("RMSE", "MAE", "MAPE")]
df <- data.frame(
Model = name,
RMSE = acc["RMSE"],
MAE  = acc["MAE"],
MAPE = acc["MAPE"],
stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)
rownames(df) <- NULL
return(df)
}))
rownames(accuracy_table) <- NULL
accuracy_table
# I choose MAPE as my accuracy measure because it is scale-independent due to this reason we can focus on relative errors based on the initial levels for the data set making it the best measure to be considered. Based on the summary table, the Moving Average Method of the order 9 has the lowest MAPE making it the best for short-term forecast otherwise Simple Smoothing would be the best in order to forecast long-term trend and seasonality and the Holt-Winters Method has the largest MAPE making it the worst forecast for this data.
# Conclusion
# The time series has a steady trend and stable seasonality, where there are stable periods of high and low total car sales. For short-term future planning, the Moving Average forecast of the order 9 should be used in order to conduct resource allocation which can prevent wasted resources in low-demand/high-demand months appropriately and ensures inventory planning for the upcoming year. For long-term strategic planning, the Simple Smoothing model should be used to understand the potential growth/decline over months/years taking trend and seasonality out of consideration because of its stable nature. This can be used to make any type of new car model planning/manfacturing or improvements to the existing models.
# As the total car sales are rising with each year, over the next year I anticipate for the value of the time series to go up. In 2 years, I also anticipate for the value to continue going up as more companies are innovating newer gas, hybrid and electric car models and increased amounts of people moving towards wanting to own a car.
# Based on the historical values, the forecasting model for this dataset from highest to lowest accuracy are: 1. Moving Average Order 9, 2. Moving Average Order 6, 3. Moving Average Order 3, 4. Simple Smoothing, 5. Naive, and 6. Holt-Winters.
